sh for your entire  life you will be taught how to design an  architecture school so if you don't know  the actual principles of fundamental  design that's okay you will be taught it  however what you won't be taught is 3d  modeling and 3d design so you're going  to have to go out on your own time spend  your own money figure out how you can  actually design something in a 3d  software package so for example if  you're learning archicad you need to  learn all of that on your own the  university most likely won't teach you  anything of the fundamental basics so  you have to go out and invest your own  time and energy into the 3d software the  3d rendering and you're going to have to  learn it extremely fast what you'll  usually see is architecture school is  filled with nice 3d models now most of  the time most lecturers during the very  early years of your degree will make you  make these models by hand you'll be  usually using some sort of soft wood  material plastics or anything in between  sometimes towards the end of your degree  they will let you 3d print a model just  to speed up the efficiency and get those  ideas across however most of the time  you will have to make something by hand  to just genuinely teach you the art and  value of model making and how much it  can add value to your overall design  those three years will go by incredibly  quickly mainly because you're extremely  sleep deprived and have no perception of  time anymore but more so for the fact  that if this is something that you truly  enjoy you will genuinely just enjoy  those three years through university and  you won't really be thinking about the  tough times that you've gone through in  australia however those three years as  an undergraduate degree you'll feel all  great you'll go to your graduation  ceremony you'll throw your hat up in the  air but unfortunately that degree means  absolutely nothing you're going to need  to spend a little bit more time at  university and a little bit more time in  the real world before you can call  yourself an architect so i do suggest  that you go straight into your master's  degree which is another two years after  your undergraduate degree for a  magnitude of reasons first of all you're  carrying through all those friendships  that you've built over the last three  years and second of all you're not  tempted to just go into the workforce  and keep working for that larger amount  of money up front but that less amount  of money in the long run during these  two years i call this practical  architecture and something that's  related a little bit more back to the  real world you'll be spending two years  on one project you'll be writing a  thesis you'll be not sleeping like  always and you will learn all the  practical skills that you need to be a  hirable grad when you finish your  master's degree the first year of your  master's is a little less intense it's  usually the time that you actually  understand what's going on you figure  out the practicalities of design and you  get your workflow streamlined relatively  well so you still might not be getting  enough sleep you'll still be spending  most of your time at university but  overall you will understand the  fundamental basics of the last three  years the final year of your master's  degree is generally a thesis project  which means you have to investigate and  explore a project in great great detail  now every single university is different  every single campus is different but  overall you will be focusing all of your  energy and all of your attention on this  one project which is really good because  this is very similar to how the real  world and real practices work you're  often working on one project for a  prolonged period of time you're not  doing tens of thousands of little  projects over the course of six months  so this will give you a really good  indication of what it's like to work on  one big project for a very long and  extended period of time now even when  you finish your master's degree you're  still going to need that shield so don't  go out and throw that shield away the  rest of your career is still going to be  bombarded with critics and people who  don't like your design so if you're not  used to your ego being bruised a little  bit you're definitely going to need to  keep that shield for a very long time to  come hopefully you'll finish off that  degree get a really good grade throw  your hat up in the air for the second  time but you still won't be able to call  yourself an architect in australia you  need to work for a minimum two years  full time which is equivalent to 3 300  hours of logbook experience broken down  into a number of different categories  you will need to sit a multiple choice  exam and you will need to sit an  interview exam but overall the process  from start to finish from becoming an  architect is seven years at an absolute  minimum unfortunately the reality in  australia is it takes architects an  average of seven years after they  graduate university to become a  registered architect which means on  average it takes australians 12 years to  become registered architects for me  personally it took seven and a half  years for some other people it might  take 20 years it all depends on you it  depends how much you want to work how  much effort you want to put into it and  seven years is the minimum so make sure  you keep that in mind the truth about  architecture is architecture is  incredibly hard there is almost a 95  dropout rate from day one to the day you  graduate your master's degree everybody  on campus thinks you're the crazy guys  on the campus because you're always  walking around like a zombie you're  always sitting in front of your computer  modeling designing rendering you're  going to be up to all hours of the night  just doing project after project it is a  grueling experience but it is an  extremely rewarding time in your life  you will develop some of the best  friendships here you will develop some  of the best memories and it is overall  worth it in my personal opinion and  that's it for me today guys thank you so  much for watching i hope you enjoyed the  video if you did make sure you smash  that like button hit the subscribe  button and like always i'll see you next  monday


